{"statement_verdicts":[{"obj_id":"lem:duality","verdict":"matched","note":"The Lean statement is equivalent to the paper's lemma. The setting matches: M is a SphereMap, IsConnectedMap gives 'connected', and FiniteMap realizes pinned reading ass:bk-1 ('M is finite'). dualRotation keeps M.Dart and edgeInv, so dual edges are identified with primal edges and Sᶜ really is the set of duals of the complementary edges. The first conjunct says complement of a spanning tree is a spanning tree of M*, where IsSpanningTree means acyclic (no closed trail inside S) and spanningGraph connected. The second conjunct says complement of an acyclic edge set (the paper's spanning forest) spans a connected subgraph of M*. Both directions hold."}, {"obj_id":"prop:pairs","verdict":"matched","note":"The Lean is equivalent to, or slightly stronger than, the paper's proposition. Hypotheses: T is a sphere triangulation with a partition coloring, as the setting requires, and PairsNoSeparatingTriangle (no 3-cycle with vertices strictly on both sides) is ass:pairs-no-separating. hpin (SingletonTreeEdgeless) realizes pinned reading ass:pin-4; it is also provably true, since every SimpleGraph Unit is ⊥, which is an edgeless tree, so it narrows nothing. The outer face t0 is universally quantified and normalizedColoring makes it black, which matches def:roots ('exchange colors if necessary'). Quantifying over every t0 makes the Lean stronger than the paper's fixed t0. Conclusion: there exist states r and s with r(t) ≠ s(t) for every t in A, which is the paper's 'pair'."}, {"obj_id":"lem:disk","verdict":"matched","note":"The Lean is equivalent to the paper's lemma. r is a State (a bijective, incident map from A to X, with A = black faces other than t0 under the normalized coloring). EdgeChoice picks, in each t in A, another vertex of t, and ChosenEdges is the corresponding set of darts directed from r(t). C ranges over all simple directed cycles (non-empty closed trails with distinct edges and distinct vertices) whose darts are all chosen. The conclusion has three parts, all matching the paper: (1) C avoids the roots (the vertices of t0); (2) the delta-sum is -3 when the cycle is counterclockwise (outer face in the right region); (3) it is 3 when clockwise (outer face in the left region). delta(d) = +1 exactly when faceOf d is black. leftLocalRegion is also defined through the face orbit of d, so faceOf d is consistently the face on the left, which matches def:delta."}],"assumption_verdicts":[],"substrate_gates":[],"escalate":null}

